{"subjectId":"sql","id":"json-in-sql","title":"JSON in SQL","group":"Views, Indexes & Programmability","order":45,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Modern engines store and query JSON documents inside relational tables — useful for flexible attributes, event payloads and API data, alongside normal columns.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-down-right\"></i>Extracting values</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE app_events (\n    id      INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n    payload TEXT CHECK (json_valid(payload))\n);\nINSERT INTO app_events (payload) VALUES\n    ('{\"user\": {\"id\": 7, \"name\": \"Asha\"}, \"action\": \"login\",    \"tags\": [\"web\", \"mobile\"]}'),\n    ('{\"user\": {\"id\": 9, \"name\": \"Ravi\"}, \"action\": \"purchase\", \"amount\": 2500, \"tags\": [\"web\"]}'),\n    ('{\"user\": {\"id\": 7, \"name\": \"Asha\"}, \"action\": \"purchase\", \"amount\": 800}');\n\nSELECT id,\n       payload -&gt;&gt; '$.user.name'           AS user_name,\n       json_extract(payload, '$.action')   AS action,\n       payload -&gt;&gt; '$.amount'              AS amount,\n       json_array_length(payload, '$.tags') AS tag_count\nFROM app_events\nORDER BY id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>id | user_name | action   | amount | tag_count\n---+-----------+----------+--------+----------\n1  | Asha      | login    | NULL   | 2\n2  | Ravi      | purchase | 2500   | 1\n3  | Asha      | purchase | 800    | NULL</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>-&gt;&gt;</code> returns a plain SQL value (text/number); <code>-&gt;</code> returns JSON. Missing keys give <code>NULL</code>.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-filter\"></i>Filtering and aggregating JSON</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE app_events (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, payload TEXT);\nINSERT INTO app_events (payload) VALUES\n    ('{\"user\": {\"id\": 7, \"name\": \"Asha\"}, \"action\": \"login\",    \"tags\": [\"web\", \"mobile\"]}'),\n    ('{\"user\": {\"id\": 9, \"name\": \"Ravi\"}, \"action\": \"purchase\", \"amount\": 2500, \"tags\": [\"web\"]}'),\n    ('{\"user\": {\"id\": 7, \"name\": \"Asha\"}, \"action\": \"purchase\", \"amount\": 800}');\n\nSELECT payload -&gt;&gt; '$.user.name' AS user_name, SUM(payload -&gt;&gt; '$.amount') AS spent\nFROM app_events\nWHERE payload -&gt;&gt; '$.action' = 'purchase'\nGROUP BY user_name\nORDER BY spent DESC;\n\n-- expand an array into rows\nSELECT e.id, t.value AS tag\nFROM app_events e, json_each(e.payload, '$.tags') AS t\nORDER BY e.id, tag;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>user_name | spent\n----------+------\nRavi      | 2500\nAsha      | 800\n\nid | tag\n---+-------\n1  | mobile\n1  | web\n2  | web</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code-plus\"></i>Building JSON from rows</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT d.name AS department,\n       json_group_array(json_object('name', e.name, 'salary', e.salary)) AS staff\nFROM departments d\nJOIN employees e ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id\nWHERE d.dept_id = 3\nGROUP BY d.name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>department | staff\n-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------\nHR         | [{\"name\":\"Vikram\",\"salary\":80000},{\"name\":\"Sara\",\"salary\":85000}]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-language\"></i>Other engines</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>PostgreSQL (<code>jsonb</code>)</th><th>MySQL (<code>JSON</code>)</th><th>SQL Server (text)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Get text</td><td><code>payload -&gt;&gt; 'action'</code>, <code>payload #&gt;&gt; '{user,name}'</code></td><td><code>payload -&gt;&gt; '$.action'</code></td><td><code>JSON_VALUE(payload, '$.action')</code></td></tr><tr><td>Get JSON</td><td><code>payload -&gt; 'user'</code></td><td><code>payload -&gt; '$.user'</code></td><td><code>JSON_QUERY(payload, '$.user')</code></td></tr><tr><td>Contains</td><td><code>payload @&gt; '{\"action\":\"login\"}'</code></td><td><code>JSON_CONTAINS(…)</code></td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Array → rows</td><td><code>jsonb_array_elements</code></td><td><code>JSON_TABLE</code></td><td><code>OPENJSON</code></td></tr><tr><td>Index</td><td>GIN index on <code>jsonb</code></td><td>Index a generated column</td><td>Index a computed column</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>PostgreSQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE app_events (id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY, payload JSONB NOT NULL);\nCREATE INDEX idx_events_payload ON app_events USING GIN (payload);\n\nSELECT id FROM app_events WHERE payload @&gt; '{\"action\": \"purchase\"}';   -- uses the GIN index\nSELECT payload -&gt; 'user' -&gt;&gt; 'name' FROM app_events;</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Use JSON for genuinely variable or rarely-queried attributes and raw payloads; promote fields you filter/join on to real columns (or generated columns) with indexes.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Store everything as one JSON blob — you lose types, constraints, foreign keys, efficient joins and readable queries.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When should you store JSON in a relational database?</summary><div><p>When the structure is truly variable (per-product attributes, third-party webhook payloads, user settings) or the data is read/written as a whole. Keep core, frequently-filtered fields as typed columns; in PostgreSQL prefer <code>jsonb</code> (binary, indexable with GIN) over <code>json</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}